# Rule: React Functional Component Patterns

## Description
Establishes consistent patterns for creating and structuring React functional components with TypeScript, focusing on modern best practices and avoiding common pitfalls.

## Benefits
- Improves code consistency across the project
- Enhances component readability and maintainability
- Leverages TypeScript for better type safety
- Reduces bugs through consistent patterns
- Makes components easier to test
- Follows React's recommended patterns

## Component Declaration

### Bad Examples

```tsx
// ❌ Using arrow function without explicit return type
const UserProfile = ({ user }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{user.name}</h2>
      <p>{user.email}</p>
    </div>
  );
};
```

```tsx
// ❌ Using function declaration without TypeScript
function ProductCard(props) {
  const { name, price, imageUrl } = props;
  
  return (
    <div className="product-card">
      <img src={imageUrl} alt={name} />
      <h3>{name}</h3>
      <p>${price}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// ❌ Using React.FC (not recommended anymore)
import React from 'react';

type ButtonProps = {
  label: string;
  onClick: () => void;
};

const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ label, onClick }) => {
  return <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>;
};
```

### Good Examples

```tsx
// ✅ Function declaration with explicit props interface
interface UserProfileProps {
  user: {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    email: string;
    avatarUrl?: string;
  };
  showDetails?: boolean;
}

function UserProfile({ user, showDetails = false }: UserProfileProps) {
  return (
    <div className="user-profile">
      {user.avatarUrl && (
        <img src={user.avatarUrl} alt={user.name} className="avatar" />
      )}
      <h2>{user.name}</h2>
      {showDetails && <p>{user.email}</p>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ Arrow function with explicit typing and return type
interface ProductCardProps {
  name: string;
  price: number;
  imageUrl: string;
  onAddToCart: (productName: string) => void;
}

const ProductCard = ({ 
  name, 
  price, 
  imageUrl, 
  onAddToCart 
}: ProductCardProps): JSX.Element => {
  return (
    <div className="product-card">
      <img src={imageUrl} alt={name} />
      <h3>{name}</h3>
      <p>${price.toFixed(2)}</p>
      <button onClick={() => onAddToCart(name)}>
        Add to Cart
      </button>
    </div>
  );
};
```

```tsx
// ✅ Simple function with inline props type
function Button({ 
  label, 
  onClick, 
  disabled = false 
}: { 
  label: string; 
  onClick: () => void; 
  disabled?: boolean; 
}) {
  return (
    <button 
      onClick={onClick} 
      disabled={disabled}
      className={`btn ${disabled ? 'btn-disabled' : 'btn-primary'}`}
    >
      {label}
    </button>
  );
}
```

## State and Effects

### Bad Examples

```tsx
// ❌ Multiple related state variables that should be grouped
function SignupForm() {
  const [firstName, setFirstName] = useState('');
  const [lastName, setLastName] = useState('');
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
  const [password, setPassword] = useState('');
  const [confirmPassword, setConfirmPassword] = useState('');
  const [errors, setErrors] = useState({});
  
  // Form handling...
}
```

```tsx
// ❌ Misusing useEffect with missing dependencies
function UserData({ userId }) {
  const [userData, setUserData] = useState(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    const fetchUser = async () => {
      const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
      const data = await response.json();
      setUserData(data);
    };
    
    fetchUser();
    // Missing userId dependency
  }, []);
  
  // Component body...
}
```

### Good Examples

```tsx
// ✅ Proper state organization with interfaces
interface UserFormData {
  firstName: string;
  lastName: string;
  email: string;
  password: string;
  confirmPassword: string;
}

interface FormErrors {
  firstName?: string;
  lastName?: string;
  email?: string;
  password?: string;
  confirmPassword?: string;
}

function SignupForm() {
  const [formData, setFormData] = useState<UserFormData>({
    firstName: '',
    lastName: '',
    email: '',
    password: '',
    confirmPassword: ''
  });
  const [errors, setErrors] = useState<FormErrors>({});
  
  const handleChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
    const { name, value } = e.target;
    setFormData(prev => ({
      ...prev,
      [name]: value
    }));
  };
  
  // Form handling...
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ Proper useEffect with dependencies and cleanup
function UserData({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const [userData, setUserData] = useState<User | null>(null);
  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    let isMounted = true;
    setIsLoading(true);
    setError(null);
    
    const fetchUser = async () => {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
        if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch user');
        const data = await response.json();
        
        if (isMounted) {
          setUserData(data);
          setIsLoading(false);
        }
      } catch (err) {
        if (isMounted) {
          setError(err instanceof Error ? err : new Error('An error occurred'));
          setIsLoading(false);
        }
      }
    };
    
    fetchUser();
    
    // Cleanup function
    return () => {
      isMounted = false;
    };
  }, [userId]); // Proper dependency
  
  if (isLoading) return <LoadingSpinner />;
  if (error) return <ErrorMessage message={error.message} />;
  if (!userData) return null;
  
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{userData.name}</h2>
      <p>{userData.email}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ Custom hook for data fetching
function useUserData(userId: string) {
  const [userData, setUserData] = useState<User | null>(null);
  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    let isMounted = true;
    setIsLoading(true);
    setError(null);
    
    const fetchUser = async () => {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
        if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch user');
        const data = await response.json();
        
        if (isMounted) {
          setUserData(data);
          setIsLoading(false);
        }
      } catch (err) {
        if (isMounted) {
          setError(err instanceof Error ? err : new Error('An error occurred'));
          setIsLoading(false);
        }
      }
    };
    
    fetchUser();
    
    return () => {
      isMounted = false;
    };
  }, [userId]);
  
  return { userData, isLoading, error };
}

// Component using the custom hook
function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const { userData, isLoading, error } = useUserData(userId);
  
  if (isLoading) return <LoadingSpinner />;
  if (error) return <ErrorMessage message={error.message} />;
  if (!userData) return null;
  
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{userData.name}</h2>
      <p>{userData.email}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Implementation Guidelines

1. **Component Declaration**:
   - Use named function declarations for most components
   - Define explicit prop interfaces with descriptive names
   - Place interfaces or types above the component
   - Use destructuring for props in the function parameters
   - Provide default values for optional props in the function parameters

2. **Functional Component Structure**:
   - Place hooks at the top of the component
   - Define event handlers and helper functions next
   - Use early returns for loading/error states
   - Return JSX as the final statement
   
3. **TypeScript Best Practices**:
   - Avoid using `any` types
   - Use interfaces for defining prop shapes
   - Define types for component state
   - Add type annotations for event handlers
   - Use type guards for conditional rendering

4. **React Hooks Best Practices**:
   - Follow React's rules of hooks
   - Extract complex logic into custom hooks
   - Ensure all dependencies are included in dependency arrays
   - Use the function form of state setters when new state depends on old state
   - Implement cleanup functions in useEffect when needed

5. **Performance Optimization**:
   - Memoize expensive calculations with useMemo
   - Memoize callbacks with useCallback when passed to child components
   - Consider using React.memo for pure components that render often
   - Use lazy loading for code splitting

## Exceptions
- Very simple components with minimal props may use inline types
- For library code or highly reusable components, consider using generics for flexibility
- Test components may have simplified typing requirements

